
<p><a href="https://news.artnet.com/art-world/stanley-kubrick-photos-duncan-miller-gallery-2765046">Vittoria Benzine, at Artnet</a> (<a href="https://mastodon.social/@oliversthomas/116421240064532339">via Oliver Thomas</a>):</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>The singular American filmmaker Stanley Kubrick saw the little
details. He even <a href="https://news.artnet.com/art-world/stanley-kubrick-museum-of-moving-image-1761054">saw the future</a>. But, most of all, he saw
people, with all their quirks. Kubrick’s films, from <em>Dr.
Strangelove</em> (1964) to <em>The Shining</em> (1980), offer proof of this — as do his earliest photos, produced during the 1940s. One new
trove of 18 such images will get its first-ever outing next week,
when Los Angeles-based Duncan Miller Gallery presents the find
alongside works by contemporary photographer Jacqueline Woods at
the <a href="https://www.aipad.com/show">Photography Show</a> in New York. [...]</p>

<p>The photos are some of the earliest images that the director made
for Look. “New York’s subway trains are a reading room on wheels,
a lover’s lane and, after 11 p.m., a flophouse,” Kubrick’s
subsequent photo essay accompanying his subway visions opined.</p>
</blockquote>

